# Kadhar's Benevolence
Tamil title: காதர் கருணை

### Fiction Rooted in Fact

*[An unfinished short play]*

## Image 1: 001-4d6f0ef374.png

### Scene 1

**Place:** The exterior of a somewhat dilapidated mansion.

**Time:** Morning.

**Characters:** A gatekeeper and a gentleman.

The mansion is in a state of decay. Its surrounding wall lies split open on both sides. Since it has neither been whitewashed nor repaired for many years, its exterior is unattractive. Dust and stones cover the threshold and the street veranda. The mansion is nevertheless very large. Its condition suggests that it could look beautiful if repaired and painted.

A guard stands cheerfully at the main entrance, holding a bowl of sandal paste and a vessel of rosewater. Mango-leaf festoons and plantain trees have been tied up. Thorn bushes stand to one side.

A gentleman passes along the street. He has a stout body, a radiant face, eyes capable of penetrating anything, and a majestic gait. He glances casually at the mansion. A song can be heard from within:

“Victory! Victory! Vande Mataram!

Victory, victory, Vande Mataram!”

On hearing the song, the gentleman looks at the mansion again, this time more carefully. Seeing its great size and festive appearance, he looks still more intently.

## Image 2: 002-018a2c0e6f.png

He stands gazing at it. The guard notices the gentleman's eyes falling upon the mansion and welcomes him with a smiling face.

**Guard:** Namaste!

**Gentleman:** Namaste...

**Guard:** Come in! [As he welcomes him, he offers sandal paste. The gentleman barely touches it.]

**Gentleman:** Is sandalwood available here in abundance?

**Guard:** Plenty of it is available.

**Gentleman:** Apart from grinding it like this, what else do you do with it?

**Guard:** We grind it. If any great person dies, we use it to cremate him. We make a little oil and soap...

**Gentleman:** You make sandal oil and soap?

**Guard:** Yes, a little, not much.

**Gentleman:** Not much? Very well, why should this mansion not be repaired?

**Guard:** It must be done. They are going to do it. Go inside and see; that is what they are discussing there.

**Gentleman:** Discussing it? Who?

**Guard:** The owners of the mansion!

[The gentleman goes inside quickly.]

### Scene 2

**Place:** The inner hall of the mansion.

**Characters:** A small gathering of the mansion's residents.

One person is speaking.

**Speaker:** After so much hardship, we have brought the mansion under our control. Our mansion is very large...

**A Voice:** We did not get all of it!

**Speaker:** Never mind. It is only a small backyard; let it go. Do you know how great our pride and happiness are at having obtained this mansion? Those who do not praise this freedom...

## Image 3: 003-ca54238b69.png

[The gentleman enters.]

**Gentleman:** The entire world praises it.

**Speaker:** Namaste! Sit down. Noble men like him will remain our close allies.

**A Voice:** Who is he? He looks like a distant cousin of the man who ran away.

**Speaker:** We must no longer speak like that. We must not subject our mansion to quarrels and disputes again. He is...

**Gentleman:** A human being, an internationalist, one who has come to congratulate you wholeheartedly on the mansion's happiness. I possess an unblemished heart. Kadhar is the name of my country.

**Speaker:** Our greetings to the honourable gentleman from Kadhar.

**Gentleman:** I offer my congratulations to you who rejoice in this mansion. I shall provide whatever help I can. Kadhar's purpose is to wipe away blemish and make happiness flourish everywhere. Your mansion is immense, but it lacks charm. It can be made attractive. It must be repaired, painted in many colours, and its surrounding wall rebuilt.

**Speaker:** Yes, and a flower garden must be laid out.

**Gentleman:** An orchard would be better.

**Speaker:** Does not fruit come from flowers?

**Gentleman:** There are flowers that bear no fruit.

**Speaker:** Yes, yes. To lay out the garden properly...

**Gentleman:** A suitable plan is required.

**A Voice:** Money will be needed. An enormous sum will be required to repair such a large mansion.

**Speaker:** [Eagerly] Do not fear! A great deal of money lies buried within our mansion. We shall locate it, bring out the wealth, and make the mansion prosperous and splendid.

[Many cry out joyfully, “Bravo! Splendid!”]

**Gentleman:** [Clearing his throat] It can be done. But...

## Image 4: 004-4a9b71e22a.png

**Speaker:** The words “cannot be done” do not exist among us. The heroic courage we displayed in taking possession of this mansion has not faded.

**Gentleman:** I admire that enthusiasm and confidence. But locating the buried wealth and repairing the mansion afterward will take a long time. Meanwhile, its damaged portions may decay further. If you accept Kadhar's assistance, the mansion can be repaired immediately.

**A Voice:** What if Kadhar's assistance turns out to be the white man's work?

**Gentleman:** You must forget the old fear and begin this undertaking.

**A Voice:** We know that.

**Gentleman:** Of course you do! You are great heroes. Your ancestors were sages. Here is your leader speaking; he has a fearless heart. Your mansion is immense. Ah! If only it is rebuilt according to a plan, all who see it will plunge into an ocean of joy. Kadhar regards assisting people like you as a sacred service. Unlike the white man, Kadhar has no intention of entering another's mansion and exercising domination there.

**Speaker:** That is an admirable policy. No government can equal self-government.

**Gentleman:** Our leader has said precisely that seventy-eight times. Renovating your mansion solely with treasure buried somewhere inside it would be difficult and time-consuming. If you wish, Kadhar will provide money and workers, repair it, complete beautiful ornamental work, and then feast its eyes upon you living there joyfully. That alone is Kadhar's purpose.

**Speaker:** An honest purpose, without deceit in the heart.

**Gentleman:** Deceit? That is the quality of paupers! Kadhar has intentions so noble that it would sacrifice itself to receive the affection of its friends.

## Image 5: 005-f6af34a929.png

**Speaker:** Ah! I am delighted and fulfilled. I worship God for granting us such assistance.

**Gentleman:** Good. I shall take my leave. I shall go to Kadhar, describe your unblemished heart, and urge it to undertake the great sacred service of repairing this mansion as a divine duty.

**Speaker:** I am most delighted. May you be ever victorious!

**Gentleman:** Have no doubt. In any event, if you send someone from here to Kadhar to request assistance...

**Speaker:** Ah, we shall do so! Babaji!

**Babaji:** I am leaving at once.

[The gentleman takes his leave.]

### Scene 3

**Place:** Kadhar.

**Characters:** Millionaires.

One millionaire opens and reads a letter, then passes it to another with a smile. About sixty people have assembled there. Each reads the letter in turn and rejoices.

**One Millionaire:** The envoy...

**Another Millionaire:** A clever man!

**Another:** A man of experience!

**One Millionaire:** He has handled the affair tactfully.

**Another:** If we offer to renovate the old mansion, naturally they will be overjoyed.

**One Millionaire:** Let them rejoice thoroughly. When we give money to renovate the old mansion, the mansion must be mortgaged. Later, let us see whether they can settle the debt and redeem it.

**Another:** Your plan is wrong. We must not demand a mortgage deed at the very moment we provide the money. That would awaken vigilance and fear. The deed should be prepared after the money has mounted up.

**Another Millionaire:** That is the correct idea. Let the fellow who went there return...

## Image 6: 006-efbde6557f.png

### Scene 4

**Place:** The entrance to the mansion.

**Characters:** The Speaker and a sick man.

**Sick Man:** Yes. They say that a loan is going to be taken to repair the mansion...

**Speaker:** Who fabricated that story?

**Sick Man:** A story? Sethi said it.

**Speaker:** The report is not the whole truth. We are obtaining money for repair expenses, but it is not a loan. It is a grant.

**Sick Man:** A grant?

**Speaker:** You will not understand the subtlety. Go away.

**Sick Man:** I truly do not understand. Earlier you used to say that foreigners would enter on some pretext and then impose their power...

**Speaker:** I did say so. Yes. That is an old story. Forget it.

**Sick Man:** I cannot forget it. Kadhar now says it will give a grant. I fear the mansion may later pass into its hands.

**Speaker:** You utter coward! Go away. I have no time to argue with you.

*[The play was not completed.]*

Mansion - India.  
Speaker - Congress Party.  
Gentleman - Grady.  
Kadhar - America.  
Sick Man - the poor.

We trust that the meaning of the play is now clear.

To friends who may feel angry and ask whether one can compose a play simply as the mind pleases:

## Image 7: 007-1c6aa70cb3.png

Everyone knows the “concern” that Henry Grady, the American ambassador, has shown toward India since the celebration of Indian independence. This play was based upon it. Do you say that is insufficient? Very well, here are two more pieces of evidence.

Grady spoke a few days ago:

“You may be able to develop your country even without receiving capital assistance from foreign nations. But if you do not accept the assistance of countries where capital is already abundant, your country's development will be delayed. On behalf of the capitalists of my country, I can say that many are prepared to lend money under appropriate conditions and in the proper circumstances. But we shall not go about in this country or another, pleading with you to borrow from us.”

Echoing him, a northern business leader named Sagal Sansha said:

“Foreign industrial magnates fear and suspect that the government here will take over all the large factories within a few years. The Indian government must immediately dispel this fear and suspicion.”

Is the play wrong, then?

*Dravida Nadu*, 21 December 1947.
